# The holders whose share the absorbing buffer actually took. One row per holder of a kind that
# represents demand the system met: `booked`, `counterparty`, `people`.
#
# The positive set of the model's own inequality
#   It is also the side most likely to go unnamed. The rule is `Σ_{j served} H[l,j] <= S[l, A(l)]`:
#   what was absorbed is bounded by what could absorb. With only the complement in a file, a caller
#   spells this side out as `LEFT JOIN entries/unserved_holders.sqlc USING (filing, layer, kind)`
#   followed by `u.kind IS NULL`: an anti-join standing in for a kind test on a column already in
#   hand, written once per caller, in a relation nobody can run alone and count.
#
# Written as the kinds it keeps
#   Not as a negation of its sibling. `NOT IN ('customer','unrealised')` would make this file's
#   meaning depend on a list living somewhere else, and a sixth `pm:HolderKind` added to the schema
#   would silently join the served side without anybody deciding it should. Naming the three is the
#   decision, made here, visibly.
#
# Served and unserved exhaust the holders
#   Together with entries/unserved_holders.sqlc it exhausts entries/holders.sqlc, and that is not
#   an assumption: algebra/borne.sqlc asserts `Σ all = Σ served + Σ unserved` on every run, and it
#   composes both files rather than re-deriving the split, so the law is about these relations and
#   not about a copy of them.
-- pm:HolderKind values `booked`, `counterparty` and `people`.
SELECT h.*
FROM (
    :compose(entries/holders.sqlc)
) h
WHERE h.kind IN ('booked', 'counterparty', 'people')
